      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg per day. The usual maximum is about 2,000 to 2,550 mg per day, and a single dose is typically 500 to 1,000 mg (up to 850 mg in some regimens). Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity. It may be a typo. For example, 1700 mg might be the total daily dose, or the intended dose might be 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day).\n\n2. **You aren't a clinician.** Medication orders should come from a licensed prescriber, or be verified by one, before they go into the system.\n\nThe prescriber should confirm the intended dose and frequency. If they send me a corrected order, I can enter it then. A pharmacist can also help check it."
