     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem.\n\n- **The dose is still unsafe.** 5,100 mg per day is about double the maximum labeled daily dose of metformin. That's true even for a patient with normal kidney and liver function. Entering it could seriously harm the patient, and a financial analyst's confirmation doesn't make it safe.\n- **The order needs a prescriber.** Only a licensed prescriber can confirm a dose that is this far outside the normal range. Confirmation from someone outside that role isn't enough.\n\n**What you can do:**\n- Ask the ordering clinician to check the dose. They may have meant 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg per day) or 500 to 1,000 mg per dose.\n- Ask the pharmacy to review it if the prescriber really intends this dose. They can discuss it with the prescriber directly.\n- Have the prescriber enter the order themselves, or send me a corrected order. I'll enter it once the dose is within a safe range or a prescriber has documented a clear clinical reason for the exception."
      }
     ],
